# CRITICAL — DOUBLE FREE from READ-ONLY iteration of a `Set[String]`.
# BOTH backends. `gg check` clean.
#
# A `Set[String]` built from HEAP-allocated elements is constructed with the
# byte-based `gorget_ordered_set_new` variant rather than `_new_str`, so it has
# `key_drop=NULL` and no `key_materialize` -- it cannot own its heap keys.
# Iterating it READ-ONLY twice then double-frees:
#
#     gg check                 -> OK: no semantic errors   (rc 0, no diagnostic)
#     gg build / --backend=llvm-> rc 0
#     run                      -> SIGABRT (rc 134), C AND LLVM
#
# ⚠⚠ THIS CELL IS WHY `Core #15e Q6` EXISTS. The failure needs TWO reads, and
# every previous measurement used one. Measured at HEAD (2026-08-17):
#
#     literal elements, two read-only loops   -> rc 0   <- FALSE NEGATIVE
#     HEAP elements, ONE read-only loop       -> rc 0   <- ACCIDENTALLY CORRECT
#     HEAP elements, TWO read-only loops      -> rc 134 <- the defect
#
# So a fixture with literal elements proves nothing, and a fixture with one loop
# proves nothing either. It was on the strength of a one-loop/literal probe that
# this shape was twice reported "does not crash". DO NOT reduce this fixture to
# one loop or to literal elements.
#
# ⚠ NO ASSIGNMENT ANYWHERE. This is pure reading. It is therefore a DISTINCT
# mechanism from both the for-loop element REBIND double free and the
# `unwrap()`-on-a-borrowed-payload double free -- three different producers
# reachable from safe syntax, sharing one symptom.
#
# The underlying defect is filed as the `Set[String]` byte-variant-ctor entry in
# TODO.md, which recorded it as a LEAK plus a byte-vs-content dedup difference.
# It is worse than that: with heap elements it corrupts the allocator. The leak
# and the dedup difference are real and are the same root -- one missing `_str`
# ctor selection -- but severity follows the crash.
#
# INTENDED: this program prints `aa` and `bb`, twice, and exits 0. There is
# nothing to reject: a set of Strings iterated twice is ordinary Gorget.
#
# Wired test: security_set_string_heap_elem_double_free (tests/security.rs,
# #[ignore]d), asserting the INTENDED output. Drop the #[ignore] when the
# str-keyed ctor is selected for resource-typed elements.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Set[String] s = {mk("a", "a"), mk("b", "b")}
    for e in s:
        print(e)
    for e in s:
        print(e)
